Hard/not hard is very subjective. To play any game you need to learn
it. Chess is way more difficult to learn then -say- tic tac toe, and
tic tac toe is more difficult to learn than tag. I place Nethack on the
upper scale. Simply because it is a complex interface to a complex game
world providing complex gameplay.

Your typical first games are just running around getting killed, but
eventually you read the help, you go through the guidebook, you learn
to set the options, etc. Reaching the point where you know all commands
is not fast... Maybe "hard" isn't the right word: "complex" might fit
better.

I find the comparison between chess and Nethack hard to make. Two
complete different type of games: in a roguelike where does "learning
to play" becomes "trying to master the game"? For a traditional
boardgame like chess, you have a simple set of rules and you play with
those, it's from the way those simple rules interact that complex
gameplay emerges. For a modern roguelike, you have a tremendously
complex set of rules, most of them hidden to the player - and it is
from the random generation and complex interactions between the game
elements that the game emerges. This is why I think you can't separate
learning/mastering for a complex computer game. The player doesn't even
get to see the dice, look up tables, etc. It all happens behind the
scene. Nethack -on top of that- has a lot of stuff that can only be
discovered through playing, special cases not covered in the
documentation. The guidebook doesn't tell you all you can do in the
game and it's already very long - longer than chess' rules.

To go back to hard/not hard, I do think Nethack is somewhat hard at
first for someone who isn't used to that kind of game. But of course
it's totally addictive and it is in part because it's so difficult to
learn/master that you keep coming for more.

